\title{A Functorial Comparison of Neural Observation Pseudometrics and Proof Spectra}
\author{Aristotle (Harmonic)}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a precise correspondence between three a priori different notions of
behavioral indistinguishability for state machines whose states and outputs carry
semiring structure: (i) the \emph{analytic} notion, a state pseudometric whose
zero locus records indistinguishability; (ii) the \emph{coalgebraic} notion, the
Myhill--Nerode behavioral equivalence of an observation system; and (iii) the
\emph{algebraic-geometric} notion, a semiring congruence regarded as a datum of a
proof spectrum. Our central structural observation is that, for \emph{algebraic
neural observation systems}---systems whose layers and read-out are semiring
homomorphisms preserving $0$, $+$, $\cdot$---the behavior map is a homomorphism in
the state argument, and consequently behavioral equivalence is automatically a
semiring congruence. We prove a comparison theorem identifying all three notions
on the nose, and we promote the construction to a \emph{functor}: morphisms that
intertwine the dynamics push congruences forward, identities go to identities, and
composition is respected. Finally we show that the observation pseudometric, which
is $\{0,1\}$-valued and only a pseudometric on raw states, descends to a genuine
\emph{metric} on the behavioral quotient, with full separation. We close with
applications to certified neural compression and a program of open conjectures
connecting primality, Zariski transport, and graded ultrametric refinements.
\end{abstract}

